# Add project-specific ProGuard rules here.
# By default, the build system uses R8 for code shrinking.

# Keep all data classes (Models)
-keep class com.example.offlinenotes.model.** { *; }

# Keep Room generated classes
-keep class * extends androidx.room.RoomDatabase
-keep class * extends androidx.room.util.TableInfo
-keep class androidx.room.DatabaseConfiguration
All the necessary files to build the project are now created. The only thing missing is the launcher icons (ic_launcher.png, etc.) in the res/mipmap folders, which are typically generated by Android Studio's "New Image Asset" wizard.🚀 How to Build the APKNow that the project structure is complete, here is your guide to building the APK.Method 1: Using Android Studio (Recommended)Open the Project:Open Android Studio.Select File > Open (or "Open" on the welcome screen).Navigate to and select the OfflineNotesApp folder you saved.Android Studio will detect the Gradle files and sync the project. This may take a few minutes as it downloads all the dependencies.Build the APK:Once the project is synced, go to the menu bar.Select Build > Build Bundle(s) / APK(s) > Build APK(s).Android Studio will build the project.Find the APK:Once the build is complete, a small popup will appear in the bottom-right corner.Click the "locate" link.This will open the folder containing your APK file, which will be at:
OfflineNotesApp/app/build/outputs/apk/debug/app-debug.apkMethod 2: Using the Command Line (Gradle Wrapper)If you have the Android SDK set up, you can do this without opening Android Studio.Open a Terminal (or Command Prompt).Navigate to the project's root folder:Bashcd path/to/OfflineNotesApp
(For Mac/Linux) Make the Gradle wrapper executable:Bashchmod +x ./gradlew
Run the build command:For Mac/Linux: ./gradlew assembleDebugFor Windows: gradlew.bat assembleDebugFind the APK:The command will build the app and place the APK in the same location as before:
OfflineNotesApp/app/build/outputs/apk/debug/app-debug.apk